<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n2250" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Ămīsĭa</span></b> , ae. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>Masc.</i>, <i>a river in Germany</i>,
			now <i>the Ems</i>, <i><span class="en">Tac. A. 1,
			60</span></i>; <i><span class="en">1, 63</span></i>; <i><span class="en">2, 23</span></i>; in <i><span class="en">Plin. 4, 14,
				28, § 100</span></i>, and <i><span class="en">Mel. 3,
			3</span></i>, called <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Ămīsĭus</span></b> , ii; cf. Mann.
			Germ. 419. —</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>Fem.</i>, <i>a fortress built by the
				Romans upon the Ems</i> (near the Fort Delf Zyl, in West
			Friesland), <i><span class="en">Tac. A. 2, 8</span></i>; cf. Mann. Germ.
		82.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
